  "as_of": "2017-06-01",
  "sentences": [
   "The bill would shift responsibility for goods and services tax (GST) on certain business transactions involving gold, silver or platinum from sellers to purchasers.",
   "A reverse charge would generally require registered business purchasers to account for GST, while limiting access to second-hand goods credits for valuable metals.",
   "The measures would apply to supplies from 1 April 2017, with exceptions for products sufficiently more valuable than their metal content and specified other cases."
  ],
  "changes": [
   "Businesses could agree in writing to reverse charge supplies even where the mandatory rule did not apply.",
   "The default value-added exception would require goods to be worth at least ten per cent more than their constituent valuable metals.",
   "The bill would add an exception allowing collectables and antiques to qualify as second-hand goods.",
   "The Commissioner of Taxation could exempt classes of supplies from the reverse charge by legislative instrument."
  ],
  "affected": "Businesses trading goods containing valuable metals and the Commissioner of Taxation are affected.",
  "model": "GPT-6.1 (Codex)",
  "generated_at": "2026-11-10T09:14:42.146426+00:00"
 },
 "divisions": [],
 "speeches": [
  {
   "slug": "speech-349049",
   "speaker": "Josh Frydenberg",
   "party": "Liberal",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2017-06-15",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The Treasury Laws Amendment (GST Integrity) Bill 2017, announced by Minister Kelly O'Dwyer on 31 March 2017 and applying from 1 April 2017, requires purchasers of gold, silver and platinum supplied as taxable supplies to remit GST to the ATO via a reverse charge. The bill also clarifies that precious metals are not second-hand goods, targeting tax avoidance and fraud while securing additional funding for states and territories."
  }
